  "1": [
   "Breeding bird survey of the Alder Marshes\rSurvey sites\t1\rSpecies\t1\rMethods\t3\r\rSurvey sites\rThe survey covered four sites along the river. \rTable 1: Sites, visits and counts\rSite\r\u0007Counts\r\u0007Notes\r\u0007\r\u0007\r\u0007Visits\r\u0007Pairs\r\u0007\r\u0007\r\u0007North\r\u000712\r\u000741\r\u0007Reed beds\r\u0007\r\u0007\r\u00073\r\u00079\r\u0007Second count\r\u0007\r\u0007East\r\u00079\r\u000733\r\u0007Wren\r\u00074\r\u0007\r\u0007Reed warbler\r\u00077\r\u0007\r\u0007\r\u0007\r\u0007\rAs Table 1 shows, the north site held the most pairs.\rThe north site lies in reed beds below the weir.\rSpecies\rWrens sang at every site\u0002, and reed warblers at two\u0002.\r/\rFigure 1: A wren at the north site\rGrey herons fed on the east site at dawn and flew to the heronry at dusk.\rA field note stands beside this paragraph.\rThis paragraph is on page 2. The report has tables.\rThe north site is described in The north site lies in reed beds below the weir.\rData: count sheet 1\rEnd of the first section.\f\rMethods\rEach site was visited at dawn by two observers.\r\r"
